{{Example}}
```

Open file returns a read-only File. Save as asks for a destination and returns a
writable File; Write accepts a buffer and reports completion through its callback.
This example captures text before opening the picker and keeps newer edits when
the operation finishes. File Desk adds app-private storage and dirty-close guards.
Always close a delivered File/Folder, and dispose the Picker when its owner is
destroyed. Native handles can keep files or folders from being renamed on Windows.
Close is safe to repeat. A closed wrapper can stay in your recent-files list;
the module drops its owned handle reference. Native token reclamation still
depends on ordinary garbage collection and the host's retirement pump.

Open folder returns a read-only Folder. `Folder:List(Complete)` returns records
with Name, IsDirectory and Size. `Folder:Open(Name, Complete)` opens one regular
child file for reading. Pass one leaf name, such as `note.txt`; traversal, nested
paths, folder writes and recursive walking are not part of this profile. Folder
grants are not saved across application restarts.

Every async method returns an Operation. `Operation:Cancel()` returns true once
while active and suppresses that module callback; Picker Dispose also cancels
operations and closes owned resources. A user's picker dismissal calls Complete
with nil/nil. Other failures arrive as a fixed code: Unavailable for admission
refusal, or native CapabilityDenied, InvalidArgument, Unsupported, WouldBlock, LimitExceeded, Canceled,
DeadlineExceeded, Closed or ProviderFailure. Catch expected programming errors
with pcall; calling Read after Close or Write on a read-only File throws a fixed
`[LUI:Files]` error. Callback failures are contained and newly delivered resources
are closed; raw application errors are not printed by the module.
Cancellation settles the request before native dialog teardown necessarily ends.
Starting another picker during teardown can report WouldBlock; keep the editor
text and let the user retry rather than treating it as a lost document.

File buffers are limited to4MiB, with32 provider resources/requests plus existing
native domain quotas. A folder list has at most256 entries, scanning at most1024;
oversize directories fail instead of silently returning a partial list. Leaf names
use at most255 UTF-8 bytes and portable filename rules. IO has a30s deadline and
pickers120s. These are service ceilings; editors should choose smaller UI limits.

The qualified target is local regular files. Links/reparse paths, hardlinked files,
whole-drive roots, direct UNC paths and devices are refused. Mounted/mapped network
filesystems are not detected portably and remain unqualified. Link-backed/cloud-only
documents, browser/mobile hosts and other file system profiles are unsupported.
Windows/Linux owner and filesystem tests are distinct from real picker, keyboard,
clipboard and physical desktop qualification; consult the candidate's evidence.

Save as atomically replaces one selected data entry using a new file. It does
not preserve the old file's ACLs, ownership, extended attributes or other metadata.
An observed external change refuses the save; this is not compare-and-swap against
another process racing the final rename. Cancellation cannot undo a committed
write, and an error after replacement can leave the new file in place. Keep the
editor text and report an uncertain save without claiming that nothing changed.

The candidate project checker also catches unknown or readonly keys in direct
literal `Instance.new('Class', {Properties})` calls. Dynamic tables/classes, aliases,
value compatibility and legacy single-file checking still require runtime
validation. The Properties:any declaration is not full initializer type checking.

